  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThere will be south southwesterly winds at the surface through the day \ntoday. This morning these winds will help to create some downsloping \nwinds along the eastern side of the Prairie Coteau, along I-29. The \ndownsloping winds will be gusty at time, with gusts up to 30 mph \npossible through the morning  into the afternoon as well as \novernight into Friday morning. The southerly winds will also help to \nbring warmer temperatures in today. Highs will be in the low 80s to \nupper 90s, warmest in areas west of the Missouri River. North \ncentral SD will also see drier conditions today, with relative \nhumidity values below 20% in areas. Afternoon winds will be gusting \nbelow 20 mph, which helps to reduce fire weather concerns slightly, \nthough there is still High Grassland Fire Danger Index values over \nCorson county from the dry conditions. The southerly winds will also \nbe pushing the near surface smoke out this morning, though there \nwill continue to be smoke aloft today that will cause some hazy \nskies at times. \n\nThe upper-levels of the atmosphere have northwest flow over SD \nthrough the weekend and into next week. Occasionally, the models \nhint at a shortwave moving along that flow over SD, which would help \nto support surface boundaries moving over the state and potentially \nshower and storm development. One of these shortwaves is forecast to \nmove over central and northeastern SD at some point late this \nafternoon into tonight. The warm temperatures will help to provide \nsome instability today in front of the surface trough that will \nprovide lift for storm development. Additionally, the development of \na low level jet during the evening and overnight will help to \nsupport storms or develop storms over south central and northeastern \nSD. The big question is, will there be enough moisture for shower \nand storm development. Some models have a sliver of moisture moving \ninto central and northeastern SD along the southerly winds in front \nof the boundary, while other models do not. The models that have the \nmoisture have storms developing at some point during the late \nafternoon into early Friday morning. There are low confidence for \nanything to develop because of the lack of moisture, but if there is \nenough moisture, something could always pop up. In fact, there is a \nsliver of a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) that IF any storms do \ndevelop over portions of south central SD, then they could become \nsevere. The lapse rates and shear over that area suggest that storms \ncould have hail up to 1 inch and wind gusts of 60 mph.\n\nMultiple upper-level shortwave and surface boundaries are forecast \nto move over SD into and through the weekend. These continue \nbringing a small chance for showers and storms to develop, such as \nat some point between Friday night into Saturday afternoon. However, \nit always comes back to if there will be enough moisture to support \nthe development of anything. This causes low confidence and lower \nchances for showers and storms. The next big chance for showers and \nstorms will be Sunday evening into Monday morning, with 30-60% \nchance. There is still a bit of variation in the models as to the \nlocation and timing of these showers and storms, as some models have \nthe storms developing to the east of the area and some develop \nstorms over SD. This will need to be watched a bit more in upcoming \nmodel runs to get more details on what will be happening.\n\nTemperatures will be cooling slightly Friday, with highs in the \nupper 70s to upper 80s, before warming up again during the weekend \nwith highs in the 80s to 90s. The northerly winds Friday will help \nto push higher concentrations of wildfire smoke in over SD. Though \nwith very little mixing occurring, that smoke will be staying aloft \nand create more widespread hazy skies.",
